1. LINUX COMMAND LINE
========================================================

The Linux command line is a text-based interface used
to interact with the Linux operating system.

Instead of using a graphical user interface (GUI), we
can type commands to perform different operations.

The command line can be used to:

- Navigate through directories
- Create files
- Create directories
- View files
- Copy files
- Move files
- Rename files
- Delete files
- Search for files
- Get information about commands
- Manage the system


Basic command structure:

command [options] [arguments]


Example:

ls -l


Here:

ls     → command
-l     → option


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

The Linux command line allows me to interact with the
operating system by typing commands.


========================================================
2. pwd
========================================================

pwd stands for:

Print Working Directory


The pwd command shows the current directory I am
working in.


Example:

pwd


Example Output:

/home/niranjan


This tells me that my current location is:

/home/niranjan


REAL-WORLD USE:

Before working with files or directories, pwd helps
me understand where I currently am in the filesystem.


PRACTICE:

pwd


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

pwd tells me my current location in the Linux
filesystem.


========================================================
3. ls
========================================================

The ls command is used to list files and directories
inside the current directory.


Example:

ls


Example Output:

Documents
Downloads
Pictures
file.txt


LISTING DETAILED INFORMATION:

ls -l


The -l option displays detailed information about
files and directories.


LISTING HIDDEN FILES:

ls -a


The -a option shows hidden files as well.


LISTING ALL WITH DETAILS:

ls -la


REAL-WORLD USE:

ls is commonly used to check what files and
directories are available in a location.


PRACTICE:

ls
ls -l
ls -a
ls -la


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

ls helps me see the contents of a directory.


========================================================
4. cd
========================================================

cd stands for:

Change Directory


The cd command is used to move from one directory
to another.


Example:

cd Documents


This moves into the Documents directory.


GO BACK TO PARENT DIRECTORY:

cd ..


GO TO HOME DIRECTORY:

cd ~


GO TO ROOT DIRECTORY:

cd /


GO TO PREVIOUS DIRECTORY:

cd -


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

pwd

cd Documents

pwd


The second pwd shows that I am now inside the
Documents directory.


PRACTICE:

cd Documents
pwd
cd ..
pwd


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

cd is used to navigate through the Linux filesystem.


========================================================
5. touch
========================================================

The touch command is commonly used to create an
empty file.

Example:

touch file.txt


This creates:

file.txt


MULTIPLE FILES:

touch file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt


This creates three files.


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

touch notes.txt


This can be used to quickly create a file for
taking notes.


PRACTICE:

touch practice.txt

ls


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

touch can be used to create new empty files.


========================================================
6. file
========================================================

The file command is used to determine the type of
a file.


Example:

file test.txt


Example Output:

test.txt: ASCII text


Another example:

file image.jpg


The command may provide information about the
file type.


REAL-WORLD USE:

The file command is useful when I don't know what
type of file I am dealing with.


PRACTICE:

touch sample.txt

file sample.txt


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

file helps identify the type and characteristics
of a file.


========================================================
7. cat
========================================================

The cat command is commonly used to display the
contents of a file.


Example:

cat notes.txt


If notes.txt contains:

Hello Linux


Output:

Hello Linux


CREATING A FILE USING CAT:

cat > notes.txt


Then type:

Hello Linux
I am learning Linux


Press:

Ctrl + D


The content will be saved to the file.


CONCATENATING FILES:

cat file1.txt file2.txt


This displays the contents of both files.


REAL-WORLD USE:

cat is useful for quickly viewing small text files
and configuration files.


PRACTICE:

echo "Linux is interesting" > practice.txt

cat practice.txt


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

cat can be used to quickly read and display
file contents.


========================================================
8. less
========================================================

The less command is used to view large files one
screen at a time.


Example:

less largefile.txt


Inside less:

Space     → Next page
b         → Previous page
↑         → Move up
↓         → Move down
q         → Quit


REAL-WORLD USE:

less is useful for viewing large files without
printing the entire file to the terminal at once.


Example:

less /var/log/syslog


Large log files can be examined using less.


PRACTICE:

less notes.txt


Press:

q


to exit.


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

less is useful for reading large text files
page by page.


========================================================
9. history
========================================================

The history command displays previously executed
commands.


Example:

history


Example Output:

1  pwd
2  ls
3  cd Documents
4  touch file.txt
5  cat file.txt


The history command helps me see commands that
I previously executed.


SEARCHING COMMAND HISTORY:

history | grep ls


This can be used to search previous commands
containing "ls".


REAL-WORLD USE:

History is useful when:

- I forget a command
- I want to repeat a command
- I want to review previous work
- I want to troubleshoot what I previously executed


PRACTICE:

history


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

history keeps a record of previously executed
commands in the shell.


========================================================
10. cp
========================================================

The cp command is used to copy files and directories.


COPY A FILE:

cp file.txt backup.txt


This creates a copy called:

backup.txt


COPY FILE INTO A DIRECTORY:

cp file.txt Documents/


COPY A DIRECTORY:

cp -r folder1 folder2


The -r option allows directories and their contents
to be copied recursively.


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

cp notes.txt notes_backup.txt


This creates a backup copy of notes.txt.


PRACTICE:

touch original.txt

cp original.txt backup.txt

ls


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

cp is used when I want to create a copy of a file
or directory.


========================================================
11. mv
========================================================

The mv command is used to:

- Move files
- Move directories
- Rename files
- Rename directories


RENAME A FILE:

mv old.txt new.txt


MOVE A FILE:

mv file.txt Documents/


RENAME A DIRECTORY:

mv old_folder new_folder


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

mv report.txt Reports/


This moves report.txt into the Reports directory.


PRACTICE:

touch oldname.txt

mv oldname.txt newname.txt

ls


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

mv can be used both for moving and renaming files
and directories.


========================================================
12. mkdir
========================================================

mkdir stands for:

Make Directory


It is used to create directories.


Example:

mkdir projects


This creates a directory called:

projects


CREATE MULTIPLE DIRECTORIES:

mkdir folder1 folder2 folder3


CREATE NESTED DIRECTORIES:

mkdir -p project/src/code


The -p option creates parent directories if
they do not already exist.


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

mkdir Python
mkdir Linux
mkdir Networking


This can organize different learning topics
into separate directories.


PRACTICE:

mkdir practice

ls


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

mkdir is used to create directories and organize
files.


========================================================
13. rm
========================================================

The rm command is used to remove files and
directories.


REMOVE A FILE:

rm file.txt


REMOVE MULTIPLE FILES:

rm file1.txt file2.txt


REMOVE A DIRECTORY:

rm -r folder


The -r option removes a directory and its contents.


IMPORTANT:

rm permanently removes files from the filesystem
and should be used carefully.


FORCE REMOVE:

rm -f file.txt


The -f option forces removal without prompting.


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

rm old_notes.txt


This removes an unwanted file.


PRACTICE:

touch temporary.txt

rm temporary.txt

ls


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

rm is used to delete files and directories.


========================================================
14. find
========================================================

The find command is used to search for files and
directories.


BASIC EXAMPLE:

find . -name "file.txt"


Here:

.       → Search from current directory
-name   → Search by name
file.txt → File being searched


SEARCH FOR ALL TXT FILES:

find . -name "*.txt"


SEARCH FROM ROOT:

find / -name "file.txt"


Searching from / may require appropriate
permissions.


REAL-WORLD USE:

find is useful when:

- I don't know where a file is located
- I need to find files by name
- I need to search directories
- I need to locate specific file types


PRACTICE:

mkdir test

touch test/example.txt

find . -name "example.txt"


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

find is a powerful command for searching files
and directories.


========================================================
15. help
========================================================

The help command provides information about
shell built-in commands.


Example:

help


To get help for a specific shell command:

help cd


Another example:

help pwd


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

help can provide information about shell
built-in commands.


========================================================
16. man
========================================================

man stands for:

Manual


The man command displays the manual page for
a command.


Example:

man ls


This opens the manual page for ls.


Inside the manual:

Space     → Next page
b         → Previous page
/word     → Search
q         → Quit


Example:

man cp


Example:

man find


REAL-WORLD USE:

Instead of memorizing every option of a Linux
command, I can use man to read its documentation.


PRACTICE:

man ls

man cp

man mkdir


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

man is one of the most useful commands for
learning how Linux commands work.


========================================================
17. whatis
========================================================

The whatis command provides a short description
of a command.


Example:

whatis ls


Example Output:

ls - list directory contents


Another example:

whatis pwd


It provides a short description of the command.


DIFFERENCE BETWEEN man AND whatis:

whatis
  ↓
Short description


man
  ↓
Detailed manual


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

whatis is useful when I need a quick explanation
of what a command does.


========================================================
18. alias
========================================================

The alias command allows me to create a shortcut
for another command.


Example:

alias ll='ls -la'


Now instead of typing:

ls -la


I can type:

ll


Example:

ll


This runs:

ls -la


ANOTHER EXAMPLE:

alias cls='clear'


Now:

cls


will run:

clear


VIEW ALIASES:

alias


REAL-WORLD USE:

Aliases are useful for frequently used commands.

For example:

alias ll='ls -la'

This saves time when working in the terminal.


IMPORTANT:

Aliases created in the current shell may disappear
when the terminal session ends unless they are
configured in a shell startup file.


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

alias allows me to create shortcuts for commonly
used commands.


========================================================
19. exit
========================================================

The exit command is used to close the current
shell or terminal session.


Example:

exit


This exits the current shell.


REAL-WORLD USE:

When I finish working in a terminal session,
I can use exit to close it.


PRACTICE:

exit


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

exit is used to terminate the current shell
session.


